A Trigger is a database object that automatically executes when an INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE event occurs on a table.

1. Basic Syntax
DELIMITER $$

CREATE TRIGGER trigger_name
AFTER INSERT
ON table_name
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN

    -- SQL statements

END $$

DELIMITER ;
2. Trigger Types
Trigger	Executes
BEFORE INSERT	Before inserting a row
AFTER INSERT	After inserting a row
BEFORE UPDATE	Before updating a row
AFTER UPDATE	After updating a row
BEFORE DELETE	Before deleting a row
AFTER DELETE	After deleting a row
3. NEW and OLD
NEW

Represents the new value.

NEW.name
NEW.balance
OLD

Represents the previous value.

OLD.name
OLD.balance
Remember
INSERT  → NEW
UPDATE  → OLD + NEW
DELETE  → OLD
4. AFTER INSERT Trigger
Accounts Table
CREATE TABLE accounts
(
    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100),
    balance DECIMAL(10,2)
);
Activities Table
CREATE TABLE activities
(
    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    adatetime DATETIME,
    activity TEXT
);
Create Trigger
DELIMITER $$

CREATE TRIGGER account_after_insert
AFTER INSERT
ON accounts
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN

    INSERT INTO activities(adatetime, activity)
    VALUES
    (
        NOW(),
        CONCAT('New account created: ', NEW.name)
    );

END $$

DELIMITER ;
Insert Account
INSERT INTO accounts(name, balance)
VALUES ('Ruturaj', 5000);

The trigger automatically creates:

New account created: Ruturaj

in the activities table.

5. AFTER UPDATE Trigger
DELIMITER $$

CREATE TRIGGER account_after_update
AFTER UPDATE
ON accounts
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN

    INSERT INTO activities(adatetime, activity)
    VALUES
    (
        NOW(),
        CONCAT(
            'Account updated: ',
            OLD.name,
            ' Balance: ',
            OLD.balance,
            ' -> ',
            NEW.balance
        )
    );

END $$

DELIMITER ;

Example:

UPDATE accounts
SET balance = 7000
WHERE id = 1;

Activity:

Account updated: Ruturaj Balance: 5000 -> 7000
6. AFTER DELETE Trigger
DELIMITER $$

CREATE TRIGGER account_after_delete
AFTER DELETE
ON accounts
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN

    INSERT INTO activities(adatetime, activity)
    VALUES
    (
        NOW(),
        CONCAT('Account deleted: ', OLD.name)
    );

END $$

DELIMITER ;
7. BEFORE INSERT Trigger
DELIMITER $$

CREATE TRIGGER account_before_insert
BEFORE INSERT
ON accounts
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN

    SET NEW.name = UPPER(NEW.name);

END $$

DELIMITER ;

Now:

INSERT INTO accounts(name, balance)
VALUES ('ruturaj', 5000);

The name will be stored as:

RUTURAJ
8. BEFORE UPDATE Trigger
DELIMITER $$

CREATE TRIGGER account_before_update
BEFORE UPDATE
ON accounts
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN

    SET NEW.name = UPPER(NEW.name);

END $$

DELIMITER ;
9. Show Triggers
SHOW TRIGGERS;
10. Delete a Trigger
DROP TRIGGER account_after_insert;
11. Why Use Triggers?

Common uses:

Auditing
Activity logging
Automatic validation
Maintaining related data
Automatically recording changes
12. FOR EACH ROW
FOR EACH ROW

means the trigger executes once for every affected row.

13. Trigger vs Stored Procedure
Trigger	Stored Procedure
Executes automatically	Called manually
Associated with table event	Independent object
Triggered by INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE	Called using CALL
Useful for auditing	Useful for business operations
